<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsenthai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1373–<a href="/years/1416">1416</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Oun Heuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsenthai, born Oun Heuan, was the second king of Lan Xang, ruling from 1373 to 1416 after the nobles deposed his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>. His regnal name, &quot;three hundred thousand Tai&quot;, is traditionally linked to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of his male subjects. His long and peaceful reign consolidated the administration and Buddhist institutions of the kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The king, whose name is written Samsenethai or Sam Saen Thai, was known before he took that name as Oun Hueun or Un Heuan. He was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>, whom he succeeded as king of Lan Xang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, and of Queen Keo Kang Ya, who came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> and died in 1368, five years before her son came to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Three temples, Wat Manorom, Wat Oubôsôt and Wat Xiang Kham, were built during his reign. In 1377 he married Queen Buvana Dhanipaya, also called Bua Then Fa, a daughter of his uncle. Among his other consorts was Nang Nawiangsari, or Noi On Sor, a daughter of the King of Lan Na. In 1393 he married his cousin Queen Keava Rudhi Fa, or Nang Keo Lot Fa, who was his father's widow and a daughter of King Ramadipati of Ayudhaya.</p><p>He also took as consorts Princess Nang Keava Sridha, or Chao Nang Keo Sida, a daughter of Chao Sidhakama, the Hsenwifa of Muang Lü (Chieng Hung), and his cousin Princess Nang Keava Yudhi Fa, or Nang Keo Yot Fa, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intharacha">Intharacha of Ayutthaya</a>. His wives thus came from his own family and from the ruling houses of Lan Na, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and Muang Lü. One daughter, Princess Mahakani, died before 1408 at the age of five.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many of the kings who followed him were his sons. He was succeeded by Lan Kham Deng, his son by Bua Then Fa, who reigned from 1416 to 1428. After him the throne passed among other sons of Samsenethai within a few years: Konekham reigned from 1430 to 1432, Kham Tam Sa in 1432 and Thao Lue-Sai from 1432 to 1433. Kham Keul, his son by a slave, was king from 1436 to 1438. Another son, Vong Buri, also called Sai Tia Kaphut, reigned far longer, from 1442 to 1480. Other children named in the royal genealogy include Thao Somphon and the princesses Anocha, Manora and Supatthat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samsenethai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Samsenthai</b>'s name means 'three hundred thousand Tai', traditionally explained by a census that counted 300,000 Tai men available for royal service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1394" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samsenthai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1394">What was here in 1394? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
